  "verdict": "Unproven",
  "steelman": "Proponents argue that replacing commercial health insurance with a unified public system would drastically cut overall spending by eliminating private administrative overhead and insurer profit, while using national bargaining power to lower provider payment rates and drug prices.",
  "finding": "Because this claim is a forward-looking prediction about an enacted policy reform that does not exist, it cannot be verified as an established fact. Economists and fiscal analysts disagree on the net cost impact, showing that outcomes depend heavily on payment rates, administrative reductions, and changes in medical utilization. Under illustrative options modeled by the Congressional Budget Office, national health expenditures in 2030 were projected to change by amounts ranging from a decrease of $0.7 trillion to an increase of $0.3 trillion, while federal subsidies for health care would increase by $1.5 trillion to $3.0 trillion.",
  "reasoning_flags": [
    "Forecast, not a finding"
  ],
  "newest_data": "Congressional Budget Office testimony published May 12, 2022 (summarizing its comprehensive single-payer simulation model). A targeted search across CBO releases through October 2026 confirmed no newer nationwide single-payer cost modeling study has been published.",
